 ◆ Feature exerciser — headings and prose                                       
 ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ 
                                                                                
 This fixture walks the heading ladder and the inline surface. Page through it  
 and check two things by eye: that each heading level is distinguishable from   
 the one above it and from body text, and that no inline construct vanishes.    
                                                                                
 ◆ A Setext level one                                                           
 ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ 
                                                                                
 ◈ A Setext level two                                                           
 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── 
                                                                                
 ◈ Level two (ATX)                                                              
 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── 
                                                                                
 ◇ Level three                                                                  
                                                                                
 ▸ Level four                                                                   
                                                                                
 ▹ Level five                                                                   
                                                                                
 ❯ Level six                                                                    
                                                                                
 Body text follows level six, so the two can be compared directly. A heading    
 must be more prominent than the paragraph it introduces, at every level.       
                                                                                
 ◇ The whole inline surface, in one paragraph                                   
                                                                                
 Text with emphasis, emphasis again, strong, strong again, both at once,        
 strikethrough, inline code, code with a ` tick, a plain link                   
 (https://example.com), a titled link (https://example.com), an autolink        
 https://example.org/a/fairly/long/path/segment/here, a bare autolink           
 https://example.net/bare, an email autolink nobody@example.com, a reference    
 link (https://example.com/reference), a collapsed reference                    
 (https://example.com/collapsed), a shortcut reference                          
 (https://example.com/shortcut), an                                             
 ╭ image ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮ 
 │ inline image                                                               │ 
 │ small.png                                                                  │ 
 ╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯ 
 and a hard break at the end of this line,                                      
 which continues here after a backslash break, and here after two spaces,       
 which is the other spelling.                                                   
                                                                                
 Escapes that must survive as literals: *not emphasis*, _not emphasis_, `not    
 code`, [not a link], a backslash \ and an ampersand & and a less-than sign <   
 that is not the start of a tag.                                                
                                                                                
 Entities: & < © … —  (that was a hard space).                                  
                                                                                
 ◇ Emphasis that has to wrap                                                    
                                                                                
 A paragraph in which the emphasis runs across the line break so the styled run 
 has to be split by the wrapper and re-styled on the next line, followed by an  
 inline code span long enough that it too must be broken across lines at forty  
 columns, and a link whose text alone is wider than a forty column terminal     
 (https://example.com/x).                                                       
                                                                                
 ◇ Images                                                                       
                                                                                
 ╭ image ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮ 
 │ alt text for a picture                                                     │ 
 │ picture.png                                                                │ 
 ╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯ 
                                                                                
 ╭ image ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮ 
 │ untitled.png                                                               │ 
 ╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯ 
                                                                                
 ╭ image ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮ 
 │ an image whose alternative text is long enough to need wrapping in a       │ 
 │ narrow pane                                                                │ 
 │ wide.png                                                                   │ 
 ╰────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯ 
                                                                                
 ◇ Footnotes                                                                    
                                                                                
 A statement needing a source[1], another needing two[2][3], and one whose      
 marker sits mid-sentence[1] a second time.                                     
                                                                                
